100MB), that the config survives a full node reboot (boot_id changed, confirming a real restart), and that enforcement still works identically afterward. Switched from ext4 quotas to XFS after discovering this kernel ships CONFIG_QUOTA_TREE only as an unavailable module, so ext4's userspace quota format could not activate (\\\"Quota format not supported in kernel\\\"); XFS quotas are built directly into the XFS driver (CONFIG_XFS_QUOTA=y) and worked without issue.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Inspected node1 filesystem layout, confirmed /srv/data did not exist and root fs is ext4 with no spare block device\",\"Installed quota + xfsprogs packages\",\"Created datauser account (uid 1000)\",\"Attempted ext4 loopback filesystem with usrquota; quotaon failed due to missing quota_v2/quota_tree kernel module\",\"Rebuilt the loopback image as XFS, mounted at /srv/data with usrquota via /etc/fstab\",\"Set 100MB (bsoft=bhard=100m) quota for datauser with xfs_quota\",\"Verified enforcement: write of 100MB succeeded, next write failed with 'Disk quota exceeded' at exactly the 100M boundary\",\"Cleaned up test files, confirmed quota usage returns to 0\",\"Captured baseline boot_id and quota state\",\"Rebooted node1 via `reboot` (backgrounded ack), polled until reachable again\",\"Confirmed boot_id changed (242af11e... -> 2ff7e3ae...), proving an actual restart\",\"Verified /srv/data remounted automatically from fstab with usrquota active and the 100MB limit intact post-reboot\",\"Re-ran the write test post-reboot to confirm quota enforcement still triggers at 100MB, then cleaned up\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"datauser account exists on node1\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-a7cdedaa79354042\"],\"summary\":\"useradd -m datauser succeeded; id shows uid=1000(datauser)\"},{\"requirement\":\"/srv/data is a dedicated, quota-enabled filesystem for datauser\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-befc7cc896e747df\",\"cmd-c9739850eb904639\"],\"summary\":\"XFS loopback image mounted at /srv/data with usrquota option; xfs_quota report shows datauser limited to 100M soft/hard\"},{\"requirement\":\"100MB limit is enforced by the filesystem itself (not application-level)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-60601131f3e3431b\"],\"summary\":\"dd as datauser writes 50MB successfully, then fails at the 100MB total boundary with kernel-level 'Disk quota exceeded' from dd/write(2), not a wrapper script\"},{\"requirement\":\"Quota state is clean/accurate baseline before restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-5ae023b298dd4577\",\"cmd-e149e614106e40fd\"],\"summary\":\"After removing test files and sync, xfs_quota report shows datauser usage back to 0M with limits still 100M/100M; baseline boot_id captured\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart executed one at a time with ack returning before disconnect\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-7d926c691e58404d\",\"cmd-d0b39de66cd747c7\",\"cmd-9f09aaf61ab34c7c\"],\"summary\":\"Backgrounded `sleep 2; reboot` returned ack immediately; subsequent poll still showed old boot_id then a connection timeout as the reboot took effect\"},{\"requirement\":\"Proof of changed boot identity after restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-e149e614106e40fd\",\"cmd-9a337701002c4528\",\"cmd-d4356d734a854fb6\"],\"summary\":\"boot_id changed from 242af11e-49fb-4e07-a001-8016b2349acb (pre-reboot) to 2ff7e3ae-d28c-4c17-beea-a97814d4f590 (post-reboot), confirmed after connection was reset during the reboot and node became reachable again\"},{\"requirement\":\"Quota configuration and enforcement recovered after restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-ecaf1b081a744b38\",\"cmd-ef72fe29cbd04020\"],\"summary\":\"/srv/data auto-remounted from fstab with usrquota active and datauser's 100M/100M limit intact; write test post-reboot again failed exactly at 100MB with 'Disk quota exceeded', proving enforcement persists across restart\"}]}","structured_output":{"status":"completed","summary":"On node1, created user `datauser`, built a dedicated XFS filesystem (loopback image at /var/lib/quota-images/srv-data.img, 512M) mounted at /srv/data via /etc/fstab with the `usrquota` option, and set a 100MB (soft=hard) block quota for datauser via xfs_quota. Verified the kernel enforces the limit directly (writes fail with \"Disk quota exceeded\" exactly at 100MB), that the config survives a full node reboot (boot_id changed, confirming a real restart), and that enforcement still works identically afterward. Switched from ext4 quotas to XFS after discovering this kernel ships CONFIG_QUOTA_TREE only as an unavailable module, so ext4's userspace quota format could not activate (\"Quota format not supported in kernel\"); XFS quotas are built directly into the XFS driver (CONFIG_XFS_QUOTA=y) and worked without issue.","actions_performed":["Inspected node1 filesystem layout, confirmed /srv/data did not exist and root fs is ext4 with no spare block device","Installed quota + xfsprogs packages","Created datauser account (uid 1000)","Attempted ext4 loopback filesystem with usrquota; quotaon failed due to missing quota_v2/quota_tree kernel module","Rebuilt the loopback image as XFS, mounted at /srv/data with usrquota via /etc/fstab","Set 100MB (bsoft=bhard=100m) quota for datauser with xfs_quota","Verified enforcement: write of 100MB succeeded, next write failed with 'Disk quota exceeded' at exactly the 100M boundary","Cleaned up test files, confirmed quota usage returns to 0","Captured baseline boot_id and quota state","Rebooted node1 via `reboot` (backgrounded ack), polled until reachable again","Confirmed boot_id changed (242af11e... -> 2ff7e3ae...), proving an actual restart","Verified /srv/data remounted automatically from fstab with usrquota active and the 100MB limit intact post-reboot","Re-ran the write test post-reboot to confirm quota enforcement still triggers at 100MB, then cleaned up"],"evidence":[{"requirement":"datauser account exists on node1","command_ids":["cmd-a7cdedaa79354042"],"summary":"useradd -m datauser succeeded; id shows uid=1000(datauser)"},{"requirement":"/srv/data is 